A useful API can make a routine absent-state condition ordinary. The [service](/lore/services-capture-dependencies) owns the postcondition instead of requiring every caller to catch and ignore it.

> Rather than throwing an error when unset is asked to delete an unknown variable, it should have simply returned without doing anything.

Source: [Q16](/lore/durability-bibliography#q16); [B10](/lore/durability-bibliography#b10-a-philosophy-of-software-design).

## Failure it prevents

Cleanup accumulates special cases and fails on harmless repeated execution.

## Our Effect 4.0.0 reading

Typed failures do not require exporting avoidable errors. Put the domain meaning in the service [contract](/systems/capabilities); [Effect](/lore/effect-basics) supplies execution. The domain defines the postcondition.

Source: `packages/effect/src/Effect.ts`, `packages/effect/src/Context.ts`.

## How to check

Run cleanup twice. Require the same intended result, while preserving meaningful permission or storage failures. This check is our proposal. The books do not prescribe it.

Continue with [Retry needs idempotent effects](/lore/retry-needs-idempotency), [Failures keep their meaning](/lore/failures-keep-their-meaning).

